    </item>

    <item>
      <title>Here’s what you need to know about the B.C. oil tanker moratorium</title>
      <link>https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/bc-oil-tanker-ban-explained-9.7016899?cmp=rss</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.cbc.ca/news/canada/british-columbia/bc-oil-tanker-ban-explained-9.7016899?cmp=rss</guid>
      <description><![CDATA[<img src='https://i.cbc.ca/ais/81ffdcf2-05cc-41ce-b462-84da5356a1c4,1765835028941/full/max/0/default.jpg?im=Crop%2Crect%3D%280%2C290%2C5568%2C3132%29%3BResize%3D%28620%29' alt='An aerial view of an oil tanker anchored on the water. ' width='620' height='349' title='A tanker is anchored in Burrard Inlet just outside of Burnaby, B.C., on Friday, Nov. 25, 2016. The Federal Government is expected to announce their decision if they will allow the expansion of the pipeline or not any day.'/><p>For years, oil tanker traffic has been prohibited off the coast of northern British Columbia in order to protect environmentally sensitive coastlines from disaster. But the federal government is now open to the idea of changing its moratorium — something the B.C. government and First Nations vehemently oppose.</p>]]></description>
